          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div class="post-content">
          <h1 class="section-title" style="text-align: left;">Shell Heredocs - for Fun and Profit, in Bash and Beyond</h1>
          <p class="post-meta">Published on June 20, 2023 • Category: Tech</p>

          <p>Shell heredocs are a powerful feature in Bash and other Unix-like shells that allow you to pass multi-line strings to commands. They are incredibly useful for creating configuration files, generating reports, and automating complex tasks.</p>

          <h2>Basic Syntax</h2>
          <p>The basic syntax for a heredoc is:</p>
          <pre style="background: var(--color-bg-alt); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 4px; overflow-x: auto;" tabindex="-1"><code>command &lt;&lt; DELIMITER
content
DELIMITER</code></pre>
          <p>The <code>DELIMITER</code> can be any string, but it's common to use <code>EOF</code> (End Of File).</p>

          <h2>Example: Creating a Configuration File</h2>
          <pre style="background: var(--color-bg-alt); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 4px; overflow-x: auto;" tabindex="-1"><code>cat &lt;&lt; EOF > config.txt
server=localhost
port=8080
debug=true
EOF</code></pre>
          <p>This command creates a file named <code>config.txt</code> with the specified content.</p>

          <h2>Benefits</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Readability:</strong> Heredocs make scripts easier to read and maintain.</li>
            <li><strong>Efficiency:</strong> They reduce the need for multiple echo statements.</li>
            <li><strong>Flexibility:</strong> You can use variables within heredocs for dynamic content.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Conclusion</h2>
          <p>Mastering shell heredocs can significantly improve your productivity when writing shell scripts. They are a simple yet powerful tool that every developer should have in their toolkit.</p>

